# Reader-side audience curation: how far up does it scale?

**Method note:** WebSearch, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar were all budget-exhausted/rate-limited. WebFetch is additionally **blocked on web.archive.org** (curl worked instead). Everything below came from arXiv API, Crossref, HN Algolia, FediDB API, direct WebFetch, and curl'd Wayback. Two subagents covering sections 1 and 4 are **still running and have not reported** — those sections are correspondingly thin.

---

## 1. The headline numbers — Bluesky custom feeds

This is the strongest evidence in the whole brief, and it is discouraging for the primitive.

**"Bluesky: Network Topology, Polarization, and Algorithmic Curation"** — arXiv [2405.17571](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17571), first posted 2024-05-27 (v3). Full text verified verbatim from `ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2405.17571`, not just the abstract:

> "In total, 39,639 feeds have been created by 18,352 active users showcasing the breadth of content curation algorithms available to users on Bluesky and the broad usage of this novel feature."

> "In our dataset, 139,033 Users have used this feature and liked feeds 295,902 times."

> **"However, in total, only 139 thousand out of 5 million users liked at least one feed."**

That is **~2.8%**. And the authors' own framing in the abstract:

> "We analyze all feeds and find that while a large number of custom feeds have been created, **users' uptake of them appears to be limited**."

Distribution shape, from their Table 3: likes-per-feed mean μ=2.128, max M=1,799, power-law exponent **α=3.413**; skewness γ=203.3, kurtosis β=60,657. The top feeds table: `For You` 16,132 likes · `OnlyPosts` 5,137 · `Science` 4,972 · `Adult Content` 4,180 · `Art` 3,268 · `New` 3,221 · `Mutuals` 3,103.

**Caveats I want to be honest about:** "liked a feed" is a *proxy* for adoption, not subscription — bookmarks/pins are private on Bluesky and therefore unmeasurable from outside. So 2.8% is a floor, not the true switch rate. Confidence in the number itself: **high** (verified in raw source text). Confidence that it means "97% never leave the default": **medium** — the proxy is weak, and the data is from early-2024 Bluesky at 5M users.

**Supply-side concentration** — "Looking AT the Blue Skies of Bluesky", arXiv [2408.12449](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12449), 2024-08-22:

> "As of 2024-04-30, the network contains 40,398 reachable Feed Generators" — but "3,782 (9.4%) have never curated any posts, while 8,792 (21.8%) did not curate posts in the last month."

> "Skyfeed, Bluefeed, and Goodfeeds, collectively host **95.8% of all Feed Generators**." Skyfeed alone hosts 85.86%.

> Labelers: "As of 2024-05-01, we identify **62 unique labeling services. However, only 46 issued at least one label**."

So the "marketplace of algorithms" is, at the infrastructure layer, three companies — and the reader-side moderation marketplace is 46 live labelers for tens of millions of users. Confidence: **high** (WebFetch of ar5iv full text; not independently re-verified in raw source).

**Many feeds ≠ pluralism** — "Whose Posts Get Ranked: Identical-Text Exposure Gaps in Bluesky Custom Feeds", arXiv [2608.13879](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13879), 2026-08-14:

> "in 33% of sets, one copy appears on the list while another does not... A new author with more followers than the competing author still loses 74% of head-to-head comparisons."
> **"These results are early evidence that access to many independent feeds is not enough to give identical texts equal exposure."**

Their scale reference: "roughly 40,000 feeds from 18,000 creators coexist on the platform" (citing Balduf et al. 2024), sampled to 1,366 public feeds, 297,093 snapshots Feb–Apr 2026. Sampling criteria are **not stated in the paper** — a methodological gap worth noting.

Related: [2608.13874](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13874) (same team, same date) runs a 5,000-feed panel, 17.8M public posts, 1.865M post–feed return records.

**Bluesky's own numbers: a negative result.** I read both transparency reports in full — [2024 moderation report](https://bsky.social/about/blog/01-17-2025-moderation-2024) (2025-01-17) and [2025 transparency report](https://bsky.social/about/blog/01-29-2026-transparency-report-2025) (2026-01-29). They are dense with numbers (41.41M users end-2025, 16.49M labels, 9.97M reports, 4,327 verified accounts) and contain **no data whatsoever on custom-feed adoption, feed-switch rates, or third-party labeler subscription**. Bluesky publishes moderation volume but not algorithmic-choice uptake. Confidence: **high**. Whether that silence is incidental or intentional, I can't say.

The [Jan 2026 "What's next" post](https://bsky.social/about/blog/01-26-2026-whats-next-at-bluesky) says: "Custom feeds are already one of our most differentiated features... But we've barely scratched the surface of what's possible there." And, on defaults: "The Discover feed has come a long way, but there's more to do."

**The original pitch**, for contrast — Jay Graber, "Algorithmic choice", 2023-03-30, via Wayback capture **2024-01-19** (`web.archive.org/web/20240119082104/https://blueskyweb.xyz/blog/3-30-2023-algorithmic-choice`):

> "We aim to replace the conventional 'master algorithm,' controlled by a single company, with an open and diverse 'marketplace of algorithms.'"
> "For users, the ability to customize their feed will give them back control of their most valuable resource: their attention."

Third-party datapoint: [TechCrunch, 2025-01-31](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/custom-feed-builder-graze-is-building-a-business-on-bluesky-and-investors-are-paying-attention/) — Graze, the largest independent feed-builder business, reaches "hundreds of thousands of unique people every day" against Bluesky's then-"30.3 million users". Order of magnitude ~1%. Confidence: **medium** (a founder's own framing, no methodology).

**Starter packs — the closest live analogue to your primitive.** A sender publishes a curated set; readers adopt it wholesale. "Bootstrapping Social Networks: Lessons from Bluesky Starter Packs", arXiv [2501.11605](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.11605), 2025-01-20. Verified verbatim in raw source:

> "By the end of 2024, 238.51×10³ (**0.95%** of all) users had created at least one starter pack, 1.56×10⁶ (**6.25%**) users were members of at least one starter pack, and 1.1×10⁶ (**4.37%**) users had employed the follow all operation... This indicates that the new feature was used by a relatively small portion of users."

> "the distribution is highly skewed, with the **top 20% of starter packs created 97.17% of all starter-pack-induced follow edges**. The most popular starter pack... with 7.01M follow edges created lists 'pro-democracy accounts'."

But — and this cuts the other way — curated sets were *enormously* load-bearing where used: "starter packs created a total of 308.57×10⁶ unique edges... a remarkable **19.95% of all follow edges of the network**", "responsible for up to **43% of daily follow operations** at their peak", and "by the end of 2024, **93.82%** of them are still present."

Read together: low adoption, extreme concentration, but the curated sets that *are* adopted durably restructure the graph. Confidence: **high** (verified in raw text).

Corroborating scale: "A Blue Start", arXiv [2505.11608](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11608) — 39.7M accounts, 2.4B following relationships, 365.8K starter packs. And [2608.17489](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17489), 2026-08-18: 50K English packs, median 37 / mean 40.2 users per pack, **more than half auto-generated personal packs** (29,874 of ~50,289), inclusion worth "about 2.00× more reposts per post compared with matched controls".

---

## 2. Mastodon centralization — measured

**"Challenges in the Decentralised Web: The Mastodon Case"**, IMC 2019, DOI [10.1145/3355369.3355572](https://doi.org/10.1145/3355369.3355572), arXiv [1909.05801](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.05801). Via ar5iv full text:

> "10% of instances host almost half of the users" · "top 5% of all instances have **90.6% of all users**" · "top 5% of instances accumulating **94.8% of toots**"
> "outages in just **10 instances could remove 62.69% of global toots**"
> "top three ASes account for almost two thirds (**62%**) of all global users" · "**89.1% of all toots** reside on instances in Japan, the US, and France" · Amazon alone hosted "more than 30% of all users"
> "**21.3% of instance went offline and never came back online**" · "11% of instances are inaccessible more than half of the time"

Confidence: **high** on the quotes (ar5iv full text); **medium** that they're transcribed with exact section context, since I read them through a summarizer rather than grepping raw.

**I re-measured this myself today.** FediDB API, `api.fedidb.org/v1/stats` and `/v1/servers`, retrieved **2026-09-04**:

- Fediverse-wide: **14,384,863 users** across **42,849 instances**, MAU **1,146,570**
- Top 40 instances: **9,444,946 users = 65.7%** of all users; **643,616 MAU = 56.1%** of all MAU
- `mastodon.social` alone: **3,385,335 users (23.5%)**, **262,400 MAU (22.9%)**

Seven years on, one instance is nearly a quarter of the entire fediverse. Confidence: **high** on the arithmetic; **medium** on FediDB's coverage completeness (it's a crawler, and instances can block it).

**"Measuring Centralization of Online Platforms Through Size and Interconnection of Communities"**, arXiv [2307.15027](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15027), 2023-07-27 — compares Mastodon, git servers, BitChute, **Usenet**, Voat. Finds "an effectively centralized use of Mastodon servers" and that "Usenet has a consistently higher disruption than the Penumbra" (git). Its methodological point is sharp and relevant: **"community size distribution is insufficient to describe the structure of a network"** — Voat had extreme size skew but decentralized *influence*, because its big communities were insular. Confidence: **medium** — the paper reports disruption curves (DAUC), not scalar scores, so there's no single number to quote.

**Nostr** — "An Empirical Analysis of the Nostr Social Network", arXiv [2402.05709](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05709), 2024-02-08: "Nostr achieves **superior decentralization compared to traditional Fediverse applications**. However, relay availability remains a challenge, where **financial sustainability (particularly for free-to-use relays) emerges as a contributing factor**." A genuine counter-datapoint to the recentralization thesis — with the cost caveat attached. I did **not** reach relay-share-of-traffic numbers.

**Mastodon *relay* usage specifically: NOT REACHED.** I probed arXiv (`all:Mastodon AND all:relay` → zero hits), HN, and FediDB. No measurement of ActivityPub relay concentration found. Negative result.

**Bluesky mute/moderation-list usage: NOT REACHED as a subscription number.** The closest is [2505.01174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01174) (2025-05-02): 3,278,406 blocks by 1,979,713 unique users over three months, median 2 blocks per blocking user, outliers over 74,000 blocks. The paper notes moderation lists exist via Ozone but reports no subscription figures.

---

## 3. Zulip — partial

My subagent on this is still running. What I have directly:

[zulip.com/why-zulip](https://zulip.com/why-zulip/): "In Zulip, **channels** determine who gets a message. Each conversation within a channel is labeled with a **topic**." Testimonial: "Using Zulip significantly increases the size of the team for which a manager can meaningfully know what's going on." (Gaute Lund, iDrift AS) and "With Zulip, I can manage **hundreds of participants across two communities** extremely efficiently, and I don't feel stressed." (Dan Allen, Asciidoctor)

The best single datapoint is Tim Abbott (Zulip founder) on HN, item [23581587](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=23581587), 2020-06-20 — full comment text via the Firebase API:

> "The main goal of Zulip's interaction design is to make skimming hundreds or thousands of Zulip messages (and replying thoughtfully to the ones where you can add something!) pleasant... Personally, **I read and respond to 500-1000 Zulip messages sent by dozens of people across 50+ topics every single day**, and I get compliments on how responsive I am."

> "it's very inefficient to do asynchronous communication at scale in Slack or any of its copycats... it's miserable to make sure you don't miss things in busy slack channel, so everyone does."

This is the one concrete claim I found about *how far one reader's curated attention actually reaches*: ~50 topics/day, self-reported, by the person with the strongest incentive to report it high. Confidence in the quote: **high**. Confidence it generalizes: **low** — n=1, self-report, founder.

**Slack/Discord/Matrix comparative material: NOT REACHED by me** — delegated and still pending.

---

## 4. The ancestors — NOT REACHED

Mailing lists (LISTSERV/Mailman/majordomo), Usenet moderation and killfiles, Eternal September, the Usenet spam collapse: **delegated to a still-running subagent; I have only Wikipedia summaries**, which I don't consider citable evidence for a design brief. I confirmed the killfile lineage (first implemented in Larry Wall's `rn`) and Eternal September's dating (late 1993/early 1994) but obtained **no traffic-volume, spam-share, or moderation-load numbers**. My one attempt at the primary Bluesky 2023 custom-feeds post via Wayback returned gzip-mangled bytes and I did not retry before wrapping up.

## 5. Dunbar / defaults / choice overload — NOT REACHED

Delegated and still running. I confirmed only that Gonçalves/Perra/Vespignani exists and is open-access: **PLoS ONE 2011, DOI [10.1371/journal.pone.0022656](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0022656)**. I did not retrieve its measured ceiling, its dataset size, or any of the follow-ups/refutations (Lindenfors et al. 2021, Saramäki PNAS 2014, Arnaboldi ego-network layers), nor any default-effect or choice-overload material. Treat all of section 4 of the brief as **open**.

---

## What the reached evidence actually says

Three independent measurements of *different* curated-set mechanisms on the *same* platform converge on the same shape:

| mechanism | ever used it | concentration |
|---|---|---|
| custom feeds (liked ≥1) | **2.8%** of 5M users | α=3.413 power law |
| starter packs (follow-all) | **4.37%** of users | top 20% → **97.17%** of edges |
| feed-generator *hosting* | — | top 3 hosts → **95.8%** |

And Mastodon, with a seven-year measurement baseline and an entirely different architecture, lands in the same place: top 5% of instances → 90.6% of users in 2019; one instance → 23.5% of the fediverse in 2026.

The primitive demonstrably works at the scale it was designed for — one person's contact list. Above that, every measured instance shows the same two-part failure: **a small minority ever exercises the choice, and the minority that does concentrates onto a few sets.** The 2026 exposure-gap paper adds the sharpest version of the finding — even holding text identical, 40,000 feeds did not produce even exposure. Availability of choice and exercise of choice are separate quantities, and only the first one scales.

The counterweight worth keeping: starter packs, adopted by only 4.37% of users, still produced **19.95% of all follow edges** and **43% of daily follows at peak**, with 93.82% persistence. Low adoption is not the same as low impact. A curated set that lands can restructure a graph far beyond its subscriber count.

**Confidence overall: medium.** The Bluesky feed-uptake number rests on one 2024 paper using a like-proxy for a private bookmark action, and Bluesky itself publishes nothing that would confirm or refute it. If one number in this report deserves a second source before it carries weight in a design decision, it is the 2.8%.
