# Addendum 2 — Section 1: group and community addressing as a curated set

## 1.1 LISTSERV — the primitive breaking, measured, in 1986

This is the best primary source in the entire brief, because the pre-LISTSERV BITNIC model was *literally* a human curating the audience set. L-Soft, "Early History of LISTSERV" — [lsoft.com/products/listserv-history.asp](https://www.lsoft.com/products/listserv-history.asp):

> "To join a mailing list, you would write to INFO@BITNIC (which, in spite of what the name might suggest nowadays, **was a human being**) and ask to be added to the list."

> "In the summer of 1986... **Hundreds of requests were received by INFO@BITNIC every day, and it could take days or even weeks to be added to a mailing list**... the lists generated **thousands of messages every day**... over the saturated transatlantic lines (**two 9600bps analog lines**)... **the traffic from the mailing lists alone was threatening to make private email totally unusable, with delivery delays approaching a week.** Some people were starting to use the lists to discuss whether mailing lists should be allowed, adding their comments to the growing backlogs."

> "**INFO@BITNIC was now manned full-time by someone who did nothing but add and remove people from lists all day long**, and that they were still unable to meet demand."

The fix was *peering* — replicating the audience set geographically so fan-out crossed the expensive link once: "**With all the Europeans on the European peer, only one copy of the message ever had to cross the Atlantic. The backlogs melted away.**" Released June 1986; 41 sites by November 1986; 1,000th public list on 6 May 1988; automatic list-of-lists added January 1988 — **the directory of curated sets became a machine artifact ~18 months in.** Confidence: high.

**Negative result:** no Mailman design doc, retrospective, or docs page gives *any* numbers on list sizes, moderation-queue volume, or where moderation load broke. The mailing-list ancestor with the most deployed installs has the least published scaling measurement.

## 1.2 Usenet — group creation as a costed rite, and its abandonment

Primary control document, Wayback **capture 1997-08-13** — [web.archive.org/web/19970813072225/http://www.faqs.org:80/faqs/usenet/creating-newsgroups/part1/](https://web.archive.org/web/19970813072225/http://www.faqs.org:80/faqs/usenet/creating-newsgroups/part1/). Note: **this canonical FAQ is no longer served live** at faqs.org — the Wayback capture is the only working route.

> "if **100 more valid YES/create votes are received than NO/don't create AND at least 2/3 of the total number of valid votes received are in favor of creation**, a newgroup control message may be sent out."
> "**ONLY votes MAILED to the vote-taker will count.**... **proxy votes (such as having a mailing list maintainer claim a vote for each member of the list) will not be counted.**"
> "as always, **the decision whether or not to create a newsgroup on a given machine rests with the administrator of that machine.** These guidelines are intended merely as an aid."

That last line matters: **the vote never bound the carriers.** The set was advisory.

**The most on-scope ancestor document in the brief** — Big-8 Management FAQ, Wayback **capture 2015-01-18** — [web.archive.org/web/20150118063107/http://www.big-8.org:80/articles/b/i/g/Big-8_Management_FAQ.html](https://web.archive.org/web/20150118063107/http://www.big-8.org:80/articles/b/i/g/Big-8_Management_FAQ.html). A governance body stating in writing why it *cannot* treat a newsgroup as a curated set:

> "**Will the board make the people in our newsgroup obey the charter of the group?** — **No.**
> • **There are too many newsgroups... for the board to become involved.**
> • **There is no way to identify who 'the people in our newsgroup' are.**
> • **There is no mechanism in NNTP to enforce punishments on people who refuse to follow the charters.**
> • Except for moderated groups... **newsgroups have no 'owners'**."

The regime change is dated in the same document: the ~100-vote plebiscite ran ~1987–2005; on **2006-09-30** it was replaced by an appointed board. The [current process](https://www.big-8.org/wiki/How_to_Create_a_New_Big-8_Newsgroup) has **no public vote threshold at all**. Confidence: very high.

**What curating an audience costs a human** — Russ Allbery, "Pitfalls of Newsgroup Moderation", Wayback **capture 1999-10-13** — [web.archive.org/web/19991013050951/http://eyrie.org:80/~eagle/faqs/mod-pitfalls.html](https://web.archive.org/web/19991013050951/http://eyrie.org:80/%7Eeagle/faqs/mod-pitfalls.html):

> "**A moderated group without active moderators is dead.**"
> "**Moderating a newsgroup that gets more than a few articles a day requires either some level of programming... or a lot of free time.**"
> "**Do you trust the moderators? Because if they don't want to be replaced, you're going to have a hell of a time replacing them.**... **moderators effectively control the newsgroups they moderate.**"

And RFC 5537 ([datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5537](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5537)) confirms the enforcement was **one unauthenticated `Approved:` header plus trust-the-peer relay policy** — no cryptographic binding of an approval to a moderator.

## 1.3 The Usenet collapse — the number that reframes it

From [en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Usenet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Usenet) (figures attributed to altopia.com):

| Date | Daily volume | Daily posts |
|---|---|---|
| Jan 1998 | 12 GiB | 554k |
| Jan 2005 | 2.27 TiB | 7.54M |
| Jan 2020 | 62.40 TiB | 107.49M |
| Feb 2024 | 274.49 TiB | 400.24M |

But: in 2010 "the number of all text posts made in **all Big-8 newsgroups** averaged 1,800 new messages every hour, with an average of **25,000 messages per day**" — and binaries "**account for over 99 percent of article data**."

**The discussion layer stayed roughly flat in the tens of thousands of messages/day while the carrier grew ~60,000× in bytes.** Post-1994 growth is not community growth; it is freight riding a flood-fill transport with no way to say "this is not for you." Duke — where Usenet was born — decommissioned in May 2010 "citing low usage and rising costs."

**Genuine negative result:** no sourced **spam-share-of-Usenet-posts** figure for any year could be obtained through any route. Treat "X% of Usenet was spam" as unsourced. (Green Card spam is well documented — 12 April 1994, "**at least 5,500 Usenet discussion groups**", and notably they *deliberately did not crosspost*, defeating the one mechanism Usenet had for one-article-many-groups.)

## 1.4 Zulip — the load-bearing design claim

The clearest published statement of the asymmetry, tabbott on HN [19285211](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=19285211), 2019-03-01:

> "**You don't need to think about the subject for every message, because 95% of messages you send are replying to an existing message/conversation**... You can argue it's slightly more work for the person sending a message to type a topic... **Whereas what Zulip's model saves your organization is every one of the N users who can have to read the conversation needing to spend much more time and mental effort.**"

**O(1) sender cost buys O(N) reader saving.** That is the whole architectural bet, stated by its author.

And the addressing split from [zulip.com/why-zulip](https://zulip.com/why-zulip/): "**channels determine who gets a message. Each conversation within a channel is labeled with a topic**" — recipient selection and conversation grouping as *orthogonal axes*, which is precisely the primitive under design.

Capacity, tabbott HN [40287460](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40287460), 2024-05-07:
> "**Zulip on a single server with 16GB of RAM can handle 10,000s of users**... the machine is usually about **96% idle**."
> "**there are very few communities today having complex conversations involving 100K+ people**... Certainly there are Discords with over a million users, like the Midjourney one, **but most of those users are just users of the product, not having discussions at all**."

Zulip scales **linearly** in users ([requirements docs](https://zulip.readthedocs.io/en/latest/production/requirements.html): 4 GB at 25 DAU → 32+32 GB at 2000 DAU, "**Roughly linear scaling beyond that**").

**The attention datum I'd flag hardest** — [Year in Review 2024](https://blog.zulip.com/2025/01/27/year-in-review-2024/): "**@topic mentions are used 9x less often than @all mentions, and 21x less often than personal mentions**" and "Only **1.2%** of active users have changed their home view." **The narrowest addressing verb is the least used, by ~9× against the broadest** — and the default-view stickiness is 98.8%, echoing Bluesky's ~97%. From [2022](https://blog.zulip.com/2023/01/11/year-in-review-2022/): "**Stream messages were read an average of 11 times**"; pinned streams used by "only **7%** of users."

## 1.5 Slack, Discord, Matrix — the cost of belonging

**Slack** ([Flannel](https://slack.engineering/flannel-an-application-level-edge-cache-to-make-slack-scale/)): the original client materialized the whole membership set locally. Data reduction "**7 times**" on a 1.5K team, "**44 times**" on a 32K team — **the cost removed was superlinear in the audience set.** The LISTSERV fan-out problem relocated into the client. No vendor figure for channels-per-workspace exists.

**The one real measurement of channel sprawl** — Chen et al., [arXiv 1906.01756](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.01756): **4,300 channels, 8,000 employees, 3 years**. Roughly one channel per two people. But the *modal* curated set is tiny: project channels average **11.1 current members**; the heavyweight channels by membership (IT Support 177.0, Social Group 84.0) are the broadcast-ish ones, not the work sets.

**Matrix — the sharpest finding in the brief.** Matthew Hodgson (project lead), HN [25977828](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=25977828), 2021-01-31:

> "**the resource requirements depend on the complexity of the rooms its users participate in, not the number of users on the server. So a single user server can use serious RAM if that one user goes and joins a bunch of busy rooms with hundreds of thousands of users in them.** This can be surprising to sysadmins, and **we haven't figured out a good way of solving it.**"

And HN [34289826](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=34289826), 2023-01-07:
> "**if you want to participate in a room whose users are spread over 1000 servers, you'll need a server beefy enough to talk to 1000 servers every time someone says something.**"

Joining Matrix HQ took "**upwards of 12 minutes**" before faster remote room joins ([matrix.org, 2022-10-18](https://matrix.org/blog/2022/10/18/testing-faster-remote-room-joins/)).

**In a federated design, the cost of belonging to an addressable set is borne by the participant's node and scales with the set's decentralisation, not with the participant's own activity. Joining is the expensive act.**

---

# What the completed brief says

Four independent literatures, four different decades, one shape.

**The ceiling is real and it is throughput, not inventory.** Per-source responsiveness decays as ~nf^−0.79 (Hodas & Lerman). Aggregate attention is conserved. A reader curating 200 sources gets one reader's worth of attention, divided.

**The curated set works at the scale it was designed for and breaks by a specific mechanism above it — and the mechanism is not the one usually blamed.** It is not choice overload (D = 0.02 across 63 conditions, indistinguishable from zero). It is the default effect (d = 0.68, strongest through *endowment* — defaults read as the status quo), compounded by the supply-side curation cost that killed ICRA and kills unmoderated Usenet groups.

**Three convergent measurements of default-stickiness, on three unrelated systems:**
- Bluesky custom feeds: **2.8%** ever liked one
- Bluesky starter packs: **4.37%** ever used follow-all
- Zulip home view: **1.2%** ever changed it

**And three convergent measurements of concentration among the minority who do choose:**
- Starter packs: top 20% → **97.17%** of induced edges
- Feed generators: top 3 hosts → **95.8%** of all generators
- Mastodon: top 5% of instances → **90.6%** of users (2019); one instance → **23.5%** of the fediverse (my own FediDB measurement, 2026-09-04)

**The two hard architectural constraints, stated by the people who hit them:**
- The 1986 LISTSERV constraint — a human curating a subscription list saturates at "hundreds of requests per day," and the fix was *replicating the set*, not scaling the curator.
- The 2021 Matrix constraint — cost of membership accrues to the participant's node and scales with the set's *decentralisation*. Joining is the expensive act.

**And the governance result Big-8 wrote down in defeat:** "**There is no way to identify who 'the people in our newsgroup' are.**" A flood-fill transport cannot hold a curated audience; it can only hold a topic name and hope.

**Two things genuinely unmeasured**, which I'd treat as open rather than settled: (1) **no study anywhere measures a ceiling on concurrent group memberships** — three independent query framings returned nothing; the Reddit proxy suggests ~2–5 concurrent with ~40% monthly churn, an order of magnitude below the ~150 dyadic figure. (2) **No spam-share-of-Usenet figure exists** in any reachable source.

**Overall confidence: medium-high.** Most quotes came from primary sources or open-access full text read directly rather than through summarizers, and the three key concentration numbers were re-verified against raw document text. The weakest load-bearing claim remains the Bluesky 2.8%: single paper, single platform, early-2024, and "liked a feed" is a proxy for a private bookmark action that no one outside Bluesky can observe. Bluesky's own transparency reports publish extensive moderation volume and **nothing at all** on algorithmic-choice uptake — which is itself, I think, the most telling negative result in the brief.
