# Addendum — Section 4 (attention limits) and the defaults/choice-overload half of Section 3

## 4.1 Does reader-side curation scale past one person's attention?

**The source claim.** Gonçalves, Perra & Vespignani, PLoS ONE 2011, [journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0022656](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0022656). 380M tweets, 25M conversations, **1.7M individuals**, six months:

> "users can entertain a maximum of **100–200 stable relationships**. Thus, the 'economy of attention' is limited in the online world by cognitive and biological constraints as predicted by Dunbar's theory."

Critical detail: the measured quantity is **average weight per outgoing connection**, which peaks between 100 and 200 friends. It is an inflection in per-tie *investment*, not a cap on tie count. Confidence: high.

**Dunbar's own direct test.** R. Soc. Open Sci. 3:150292, 2016 — recovered via [europepmc.org/articles/PMC4736918](https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC4736918) after royalsocietypublishing.org returned 403. Two UK samples (n=2000, n=1375):

> "The mean number of friends is 155.2 and 182.8 in the two samples... Neither of these differs significantly from the value of 150."
> "the two samples provide a direct test of whether online SNSs allow individuals to have larger social networks than is possible offline... **The results clearly suggest that they do not.**"
> "Respondents who had unusually large networks did not increase the numbers of close friendships they had, but rather added more loosely defined acquaintances into their friendship circle **simply because most social media sites do not allow one to differentiate between these layers**."

That last clause is the one that bears directly on the design question. Dunbar also explicitly dismisses the Gonçalves-type evidence: those studies "cannot really be considered conventional everyday social communities in any meaningful sense." Confidence in the text: high. Confidence in the finding: **medium-low** — self-reported counts on a coarse categorical scale from a commercial polling panel, commissioned (per the paper's own Methods) by a biscuit company's PR agency.

**The refutation.** Lindenfors, Wartel & Lind, Biol. Lett. 17:20210158, 2021 — [europepmc.org/articles/PMC8103230](https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8103230):

> "Bayesian and generalized least-squares phylogenetic methods generate approximations of average group sizes between 69–109 and 16–42... However, **enormous 95% confidence intervals (4–520 and 2–336)** imply that specifying any one number is futile."
> "**Our reanalysis provides the last piece of evidence needed to disregard Dunbar's number.**"

The agent's scope caveat is important and I endorse it: Lindenfors attacks *the primate-neocortex derivation*, not the behavioral measurements. These two literatures do not collide head-on. Treat ≈150 as a replicated empirical regularity within one research program and a poorly-justified cognitive constant — different claims, routinely elided.

**The number that actually answers your question.** Hodas & Lerman, [arXiv 1205.2736](https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2736) (Sci. Rep. 4:4343):

> "𝒫nf = 0.22 nf^0.21/(nf+0.53)"

Per-source responsiveness falls roughly as **nf^−0.79** — near-inverse. **Aggregate attention is conserved; adding sources dilutes, it does not accumulate.** This is the cleanest quantitative form of "reader-side curation does not scale by adding more curated sources." Confidence: medium-high (read from ar5iv render, not verified against the published version).

**Throughput, not inventory.** Miritello et al., [arXiv 1304.1979](https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.1979) (Sci. Rep. 3:1950) — **~20M people, 19 months, ~700M ties**, the largest-N support for a capacity constraint anywhere in this set:

> "individuals exhibit a **finite communication capacity**, which limits the number of ties they can maintain active."
> "on average people activate/deactivate about **8 (reciprocated) ties in a period of 7 months**"; 20% churn more than 15.

**Churn.** Saramäki et al., [arXiv 1204.5602](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5602) (PNAS 111:942), n=24 over 18 months: network overlap between successive intervals **⟨J⟩=0.22±0.09 and 0.27±0.09** — roughly three quarters of alters turn over in 18 months; even the top-20 slots turn over ~55–65%. The *shape* of attention allocation is a personal constant (82%±12% signature stability); the *identities* filling the slots do not persist.

**Layer structure at scale.** Arnaboldi/Passarella/Conti/Dunbar, [arXiv 2206.02422](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.02422) — 91,347 Facebook and 394,238 Twitter ego networks. Twitter layers **1.66 / 5.06 / 12.87 / 32.66 / 97.47**; Facebook **4.70 / 15.31 / 44.77 / 132.50**; "the ratio between the size of adjacent circles appears to be a constant with a value around 3." Note Twitter's active network measures **97.47**, meaningfully below 150 — "coincides with Dunbar's number" is the authors' gloss, not a tight fit.

## 4.2 The cost of maintaining GROUP memberships — the gap you asked about

This is the thinnest part of the literature, and the finding is a **negative result worth taking seriously**.

Hamilton et al., "All Who Wander" (WWW 2015), [arXiv 1503.01180](https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.01180) — 157K Reddit users with 50+ posts, 76.6M posts:

> **Average communities per user: 28.85; median 26.00** (lifetime)
> "Redditors post to 5 communities in their first 10 posts and then post to **2.5 new communities every 10 posts**."
> "**Users rack up more abandoned communities than return engagements**" — communities posted to exactly once outnumber those posted to twice or more.

The design-relevant figure is not the ~29 lifetime count but the **concurrent** one implied by the window statistics: ~5 communities per 10 posts, of which only ~2.5 are repeats. That is **roughly an order of magnitude below the dyadic-tie ceiling.**

Hamilton et al., "Loyalty in Online Communities" (ICWSM 2017), [arXiv 1703.03386](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03386): median month-over-month loyalty across 1,440 communities **60.7%** — implying **~40% monthly membership churn**, i.e. group memberships churn *faster* than dyadic ties (75% persistence over seven months in Miritello). Vagrants outnumber loyal users ~11:1, yet each group contributes ~10% of comments.

> "When users have the choice to engage with a variety of different communities, they often become loyal to just one, focusing on that community at the expense of others."

**NOT REACHED, and confirmed as a real hole:** no study measuring a ceiling on the number of groups/channels/servers a person can concurrently sustain. Three independent arXiv/Crossref query framings returned zero relevant hits. No Discord-server-count, Slack-workspace-count, or Facebook-Groups-count study exists that either of us could find. **Treat "how many curated sets can one person hold" as unmeasured**; the Reddit figures are the closest proxy and come from one platform and one research group.

## 4.3 Defaults vs choice overload — the asymmetry

This is the cleanest result in the entire brief, and it points opposite to the usual intuition.

**Defaults are large and robust.** Jachimowicz, Duncan, Weber & Johnson, *Behavioural Public Policy* 3(2), 2019, [doi.org/10.1017/bpp.2018.43](https://doi.org/10.1017/bpp.2018.43) (Cambridge Core 500'd; abstract recovered from the Crossref JATS record):

> "meta-analysis of the **58 default studies (pooled n = 73,675)**... our analysis reveals a considerable influence of defaults (**d = 0.68, 95% CI = 0.53–0.83**)... the majority of default studies find positive effects, but several do not find a significant effect, and two even demonstrate negative effects."
> Defaults are more effective "when they operate through **endorsement**... or **endowment** (defaults that are seen as reflecting the status quo)."

Johnson & Goldstein, *Science* 302:1338, [dangoldstein.com/papers/DefaultsScience.pdf](https://www.dangoldstein.com/papers/DefaultsScience.pdf): opt-in 42% vs opt-out 82% in experiment; country-level "two distributions have no overlap, and **nearly 60 percentage points separate the two groups**." But the load-bearing caveat is in the paper itself — the effect on *actual* donation is only **16.3%**, not 60 points. **Registration is cheap; behavior is not.** That gap is the exact analogue of "subscribed to a feed" vs "reads the feed," and it is the single most transferable finding here.

**Choice overload is, on average, nothing.** Scheibehenne, Greifeneder & Todd, *JCR* 37(3), 2010, [scheibehenne.de/ScheibehenneGreifenederTodd2010.pdf](https://www.scheibehenne.de/ScheibehenneGreifenederTodd2010.pdf) (also Wayback, capture **2026-08-20**):

> "In a meta-analysis of **63 conditions from 50 published and unpublished experiments (N = 5,036)**, we found a **mean effect size of virtually zero** but considerable variance between studies."
> "**D = 0.02 (CI95 −0.09 to 0.12)**... I² = 68%"
> After 20% trimming: "**D_trimmed = 0.001 (CI95 −0.08 to 0.07)**", I² drops to 22%.

The famous Iyengar & Lepper jam study ([PDF](https://faculty.washington.edu/jdb/345/345%20Articles/Iyengar%20%26%20Lepper%20(2000).pdf)) turns out to rest on **31 purchases in one cell versus roughly 4 in the other** — consistent with there being no replicable average effect.

**So: any argument that "too many feed options will paralyze users" has no meta-analytic support.** The correct opposing argument is the default effect, which is separately and far better evidenced. **Caveat that matters: Chernev, Böckenholt & Goodman 2015** — the moderated, *nonzero* counterpart meta-analysis and the standard rebuttal to Scheibehenne — was **NOT REACHED**. Its absence materially weakens the strong reading. Treat Scheibehenne's null as the unconditional mean, not a settled verdict.

## 4.4 Middleware: designers already anticipated the default trap

Fukuyama et al., Stanford Working Group on Platform Scale, Nov 2020 ([landing](https://cyber.fsi.stanford.edu/publication/report-working-group-platform-scale), [PDF](https://fsi-live.s3.us-west-1.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/platform_scale_whitepaper_-cpc-pacs.pdf)) proposes to *legislate against* the default effect:

> "**Middleware can serve its intended purpose only if it is used widely, and a default option that allows users to never consider installing middleware would severely limit its uptake.**"

Daphne Keller, *Journal of Democracy* 32(3), 2021, [journalofdemocracy.org/articles/the-future-of-platform-power-making-middleware-work](https://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/the-future-of-platform-power-making-middleware-work/) — the historical precedent is the sharpest thing in the section:

> "**ICRA's third-party curators never showed up. Assessing all that content was hard and thankless, even when the internet was much smaller.**... its demise is a sobering reminder that curation costs matter."
> "We cannot expect comparable investment from a diverse ecosystem of middleware providers... **Unless middleware services can do this, the value they provide will be limited, as will users' incentives to choose them over curation by the platforms themselves.**"

And the visibility ceiling on what any third-party curator can even see — on Facebook "the average user had **53.9 friends with 'public' statuses out of 328 total friends**" ([NBER WP 25843](https://www.nber.org/papers/w25843)).

**Attribution warning I want to pass on explicitly:** Keller's published skepticism is **supply-side** (cost, revenue, privacy-limited data access), not demand-side apathy. The strongest demand-side sentence she wrote is the conditional one above. Do not attribute a stronger claim to her.

---

## What the addendum changes

The agent independently recovered the same Bluesky 2.8% figure from [arXiv 2405.17571](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17571) and flagged the same caveat I did — liking ≠ subscribing ≠ reading. That is now two independent reads of the raw source agreeing on both the number and its weakness.

The two sections now interlock. Section 2/3 showed that ~3–4% of users on the platform *purpose-built* for reader-side curation ever exercise it, and that those who do concentrate onto a few sets (top 20% of starter packs → 97.17% of edges). Section 4 supplies the mechanism and rules out the obvious explanation:

- It is **not choice overload** — that effect is D = 0.02, indistinguishable from zero across 63 conditions.
- It **is the default effect** — d = 0.68, and specifically strong through "endowment," i.e. defaults read as the status quo. Bluesky's Discover feed is exactly that.
- And the ceiling is **throughput, not inventory**: per-source responsiveness decays as ~nf^−0.79. A reader who curates 200 sources does not get 200 sources' worth of attention; they get one reader's worth, divided.

The group-membership finding is the one most directly aimed at your brief's actual question, and it is the weakest-evidenced: **concurrent group participation appears to run ~2–5, an order of magnitude below the ~150 dyadic ceiling, with ~40% monthly churn.** No study measures a group-count ceiling directly. If the design depends on people sustaining many curated group memberships at once, that assumption is currently **unmeasured in the literature**, not merely unsupported.

Confidence overall on the addendum: **medium-high** — most quotes came from open-access full text (Europe PMC, ar5iv, author-hosted PDFs) rather than summarizers, and the one meta-analysis read only via Crossref abstract (Jachimowicz) is flagged as such.
